      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I think this is the approach we want to start with, since as you noted, it most closely matches what we currently have as our policy.\n\nMy earlier statement wasn’t accurate.\n\nIn the `maven_jar`-style setup, Gerrit effectively has a single third-party resolution, so JGit ends up *compiling* against the same artifact versions that Gerrit provides at *runtime* (i.e., the versions that get packaged into `release.war`).\n\nWith a unified `maven_install` shared between Gerrit and JGit, we would preserve that property explicitly: we would define a single set of versions (the ones Gerrit wants) and ensure that any differing versions requested by JGit converge to those.\n\nWith separate `maven_install` universes (`jgit_deps`, `gerrit_deps`, ...), we instead create independent dependency graphs that may resolve different transitive versions, as demonstrated by the ambiguity report linked above. That means JGit can compile against foo:42 resolved from `@jgit_deps`, while at runtime Gerrit ships and uses foo:41 from @gerrit_deps. This compile-time vs runtime mismatch is inherently unsafe and can lead to linkage failures (e.g. `NoSuchMethodError`, `ClassNotFoundException`).\n\nTherefore, we should converge `jgit_deps`, `gerrit_deps`, and `gerrit_nongoogle_deps` into a single `maven_install` universe (e.g. `thirdparty_deps` / `external_deps`) and update JGit to reference that unified universe as well (instead of the current `@jgit_deps`).",
